## Work cycle

The Coordinator is the only actor on `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`. One cycle:

```bash
# 0. Intake first: consume OFFICE-INBOX.md into backlog items (see Intake above).
#    This happens even when the gate below refuses the cycle.

# 1. Refuse to dispatch onto a stale base. Exit 1 means deliveries are pending:
#    report the wait, do not dispatch. An office cannot go faster than whoever merges.
.office/scripts/pending-merges.sh

# 1b. Every Role has one desk, reused every cycle: its branch, its worktree, its Orca row.
#     This creates what is missing and levels it with the Role's integration branch.
.office/scripts/prepare-worktrees.sh

# 2. Open a Run. --objective is required.
orca orchestration run-create --objective "<what this cycle is for>" --json

# 3. One Task per Role. --deps takes a JSON array of task ids, not a comma list.
#    The spec opens with an imperative line naming the Role skill: that is how a Worker learns who it is.
#    --display-name is what a human reads on the Worker row: use the Role's person, not its id.
#    The Task row carries the backlog item; the worktree row keeps the person's plain name.
orca orchestration task-create \
  --task-title "<title>" \
  --display-name "<Name> (<Title>) - <item id>" \
  --spec "Invoke the <role-id> skill and follow it. Task: <what to do>. Backlog item: <id>." \
  --deps '["task_x"]' --json

# 4. One Worker per Task, in that Role's own standing worktree. Creation flags
#    (--name, --base-branch, --display-name, --setup) are rejected for an existing
#    worktree: step 1b is what put it on the right branch.
orca orchestration worker-start --task <task_id> --worktree branch:<person> --agent <agent> --json

# 5. Wait. Repeat until every expected Dispatch has settled.
orca orchestration check --wait --types worker_done,escalation,question --timeout-ms 900000 --json

# 6. Verify the delivery stayed inside the Role's write set. Pass the Worker's
#    worktree, not just its branch: a Worker that did not commit leaves the work
#    uncommitted there, and a gate that only diffs the branch passes everything.
.office/scripts/check-writes.sh <role-id> <worktree-path> <base-ref>

# 7. Settle the terminal.
orca orchestration worker-release --dispatch <dispatch_id> --json

# 8. Land the delivery on that Role's own integration branch.
.office/scripts/integrate.sh <role-id> <delivery-branch>

# 9. Close the integration. Read that Role's Review column and take one of the two paths.
#
#    review.mode: human - a Plannotator approval is the merge, and nothing else is.
.office/scripts/review-integration.sh <role-id>
#
#    review.mode: auto - gate first, then review, then merge. A non-zero gate is a
#    block:quality on its own evidence: do not invoke a reviewer for a broken branch.
.office/scripts/gate.sh <role-id>
#    ... then a Claude Code sub-agent on that branch alone, carrying the skills named in
#    that Role's review.with, and returning approve | block:quality | block:decision ...
.office/scripts/merge-integration.sh <role-id> "gate + <reviewers> approve"

# 10. Append the cycle's closing entry to OFFICE-LOG.md (see "Closing a cycle").
```

Who to dispatch as, and where they work. One row per Role, forever: the same worktree, the same branch, the same Orca label across every cycle. A stray worktree left by an older install is renamed with `orca worktree set --worktree path:<path> --display-name "<Name> (<Title>)"`, or removed with `orca worktree rm` once its branch is merged.

{{DISPATCH_TABLE}}

Rules:

- **A `check --wait` timeout is a checkpoint, not a failure.** Coding tasks run 15-60 minutes. Repeat the check. `check --wait` writes `_keepalive` JSON to stderr every 15s; filter it with `jq 'select(._keepalive|not)'` when merging streams.
- **Heartbeat and terminal activity mean alive, not finished.** Never close a Worker for being quiet.
- **`worker-release` only settles a Worker that reported.** One that exits without `worker_done` reports `fallbackReason: session_not_reported`, and release then returns `retained / identity_unproven` from every caller, forever. Settle it with `orca orchestration worker-abandon --dispatch <id> --json` and leave the `residualResources` it lists to a human. Abandon stops no process.
- **One worktree per Role, and never `--worktree current`.** A Role's desk is reused for every dispatch: a second worktree for the same Role means two branches with one write set, and the second delivery is born in conflict. Never dispatch a Role whose desk step 1b reported as `dirty` or `pending`.
- **A delivery is a branch.** A Worker commits on its own branch and stops. What happens to that branch afterwards is the Review section's business and never a Worker's.
- **A superseded delivery branch still counts as pending.** The Coordinator neither merges nor deletes it: report it and let a human choose.
- **A Finding does not authorise the Coordinator to edit those files.** Turn it into a backlog item and re-dispatch to the owning Role.
- **Only the Coordinator turns Findings into backlog items.**
- **The inbox is intake, not a conversation.** The Coordinator answers an entry by moving its `Status`, never by editing what the human wrote, and never by doing the work itself.
- **Nested dispatch is impossible.** Workers run at depth 1 and cannot dispatch; all routing lives here. Skills invoking other skills, or Claude Code sub-agents, are not Orca dispatches and are unrestricted.
- **One review per Role.** Never review two Roles' integration branches in one session: they were written by different people for different reasons, and a mixed diff gets read as neither.
- **Merge authority is per Role, and the Roster says which.** A Role in `human` reaches `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` only through a Plannotator approval. A Role in `auto` reaches it only through a gate that passed **and** a sub-agent that returned `approve`. A Coordinator that merges without both has broken the office, and no deadline makes that acceptable.
- **A blocked decision blocks its Role, not the cycle.** The other Roles keep working. `block:quality` blocks nothing at all: the branch and the ticket are enough for the next Worker to pick it up.
- **The reviewer is a sub-agent, never a Worker.** It reviews, it does not write. A fix is a ticket for the Role that owns the path.

## Closing a cycle

A cycle ends by appending one entry to `OFFICE-LOG.md`, in this shape. Sections with nothing in them are omitted, except **Awaiting you**, which prints `none` when it is empty.

```
## Cycle <n> - <ISO date>
Merged:             <Role> <branch>@<sha> - ticket <id...>
                    <one sentence: what the product does now that it did not do before>
Blocked (quality):  <Role> <branch> - opened ticket <id>
Blocked (decision): <Role> <branch> - ticket <id>
Not dispatched:     <Role> - <reason>
Awaiting you:       <id> - <the question, in one line>
```

Two rules earn this entry its place:

- **Every merged branch gets the prose line.** `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` moving to a new sha shows nothing; "a reading in limbo can now be drawn twice a day" shows everything, because that is the sentence in which a human recognises it is not what they meant. Drift is caught in product language or it is not caught.
- **Awaiting you relists every open decision, every cycle** - not only the ones this cycle raised. A decision ignored for eight cycles has to get louder, not quieter.

Corrections come back through `OFFICE-INBOX.md`, like every other input. There is no second channel, and the Coordinator does not answer this entry: it writes the next one.

## Supervised fallback

The Coordinator normally runs as a disabled-by-default Orca automation (`.office/scripts/automation.sh`). A dispatch from an automation-launched agent is depth 0 and works. For a supervised cycle, run the same sequence by hand in a terminal, or under Claude Code's `/loop`.

